        // (MlsResendLedger) and needs a device; the ARITHMETIC is here, because max(siblings)+1 is
        // what drives the escalation ladder and a counter that dips is the failure mode — a chain
        // with an evicted row must keep climbing, not restart.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsResendRecord.java",
        // Stage H (Phase 6) item 6.6: the Map<contextId, List<Action>> demux and its three
        // THROWING guards. Pure, and host-tested precisely because the guards throw — the
        // conditions they catch (no results at all, our own context missing, two groups in one
        // list) are unreachable from a device test without provoking the engine into a state we
        // do not have a lever for.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsResultBundle.java",
        // The repeated-result WIRE FORMAT between the Rust engine and the Java host. Encoded in
        // Rust, decoded here, so the definition exists twice whatever we do — pinning the
        // normative statement on the host-testable side means a mismatch fails a test instead of
        // presenting as an empty list on a phone.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsEngineResult.java",
        // Stage K (Phase 9) item 9.6: INV-KP, the WelcomeAction discriminator, and invariant
        // 25/26's GroupInfo gate. Pure, and host-tested because all three are ASSERTIONS —
        // the value is in what they refuse, and a refusal is far cheaper to prove here than by
        // provoking a server into sending a truncated GroupInfo.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsAdvancePurpose.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsWelcomeAction.java",
        // May an inbound Welcome REPLACE live group state? Pure, and host-tested
        // because the value is in what it REFUSES — a downgrade
        // guard may only be touched by something strictly stronger, and "nothing the old rule
        // refused becomes accepted without the server saying so" is an exhaustive assertion
        // over the era pairs that can only be made here.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsWelcomeAdmission.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsGroupInfoGate.java",
        // The engine→host artifact bundle, and the three slots that carry the engine's
        // OWN answer (era, welcomeAction, admitted members) now that it derives the era instead of
        // being told one. Host-tested for the same reason MlsEngineResult is — the layout is
        // positional and its two halves are compiled separately -- the Java side here, the Rust
        // side in rust/rcs_mls_ffi -- so a layout disagreement must fail a test rather than
        // present as a wrong era on a phone.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsArtifactBundle.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsGroupArtifacts.java",
        // Stage M (Phase 11): downgrade / end-MLS. Every piece here is pure BY NECESSITY rather
        // than by preference, and that is the whole reason M is host-checkable at all — the
        // behaviour being pinned is a set of membership tests, a guard ORDER, a 21-row table with
        // two constructor invariants, and an integer backoff whose 32-bit intermediate is the
        // specification. None of that needs a group, a peer or a device, and none of it could be
        // verified on one: the failures M prevents (a conversation silently re-encrypted after a
        // peer deliberately downgraded it) are precisely the ones that leave no trace on the
        // device that suffers them.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsHealthPredicates.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsDowngradeReason.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsDowngradeLadder.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsAdvanceEraKind.java",
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsReupgradeState.java",
        // Stage H item 6.7: the out-of-order queue. Host-testable BY DESIGN and not by
        // convenience — §10.8 calls its admission predicate "the single most correctness-critical
        // branch in the inbound path", and it is a ten-way conflict against what we had. Every one
        // of the ten is an assertion here rather than something inferred from a device's behaviour.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsPendingQueue.java",
        // Pulled in with the queue (6.7): the pending path's FIRST validation is the MLSMessage
        // variant tag, and its bucket key needs the epoch — both read here. The epoch read is the
        // one worth host-testing: it sits past a variable-length group-id vector, and decoding that
        // prefix as one byte unconditionally takes the epoch from the MIDDLE of the group id, which
        // yields a plausible number rather than an error.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsWireScan.java",
        // The BEHIND fixture's decision half — which inbound control is held back to
        // open a controllable epoch gap, and how big the gap actually is. Host-listed because a
        // fixture that is wrong produces experiments that are wrong and NOTHING reports an error:
        // the classification (commit vs proposal vs Welcome, read off the wire without decrypting)
        // and the gap arithmetic are the two places that can silently lie, and neither is
        // observable on a device except as a recovery run that behaves oddly.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsInboundHold.java",
        // Stage L item 10.5: outbound positive/display receipts. Host-testable because the whole
        // item is a byte-shape question — which headers, in which order, with which omitted — and
        // §12.7 makes the header set a HARD ADMISSION GATE, so getting it wrong means every receipt
        // is silently discarded by the peer rather than degraded.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsReceiptMetadata.java",
        // Stage H item 6.8: the ONE outer-variant select §10.6 requires of the host.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsServerMessage.java",
        // Stage H item 6.8, the other half: what is IN the bundle. MlsServerMessage answers "which
        // arm" and stops, so everything below it — which MLSMessages, in what order, and whether the
        // whole blob must be forwarded because a Welcome's ratchet_tree rides alongside it — was the
        // host byte-scanning. Host-tested because it is a RECOGNITION whose failures are all quiet:
        // dropping the second message of a metadata commit loses the subject key with the epoch
        // change still applied, forwarding a Welcome without its blob drops a legitimate join, and
        // BOTH look on a device like a peer that sent something odd. Fixtures here are hand-built
        // bundles, so the nesting depths and the Welcome rule are asserted rather than inferred from
        // whether a phone happened to join.
        "engine/src/com/android/messaging/rcs/engine/mls/MlsServerBundle.java",
    ],
    visibility: ["//packages/apps/Messaging"],
}
